EDMA v1 CPU port/NAPI lifetime fixture (patch 015)
================================================

Run from the source checkout, with a new evidence directory each time:

/usr/bin/python3 tests/test-edma-probe-lifetime.py --upstream-cache /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/upstreams/nss/qca-nss-dp --output /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/audits/20261030/core-risk-phase2/EDMA-LIFETIME-gcc-final --compiler /opt/homebrew/bin/gcc-16
/usr/bin/python3 tests/test-edma-probe-lifetime.py --upstream-cache /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/upstreams/nss/qca-nss-dp --output /Volumes/FX-MD87/Review-AX6/audits/20261030/core-risk-phase2/EDMA-LIFETIME-clang-final --compiler /usr/bin/clang --sanitize

The runner freshly reads exact Git pin d8f802f08fd8ff31057ba58edb20bbe448e7b505
and applies 001-015 after an independent declared-coordinate/full-context
check, then verifies the exact expected output bytes after each patch. Later
patches are excluded; this is not a complete later-candidate qualification.
Old r5 used only tool logs and did not detect a silent offset in 012; the
follow-up gate and header-only maintenance of 008/012 are separately bound.
It rejects any 015 changed path
outside the six authorized source/header files. edma_cfg.c and the actual
014 take/producer/clean functions must remain unchanged. No archived audit
program is executed; current, read-only TX test utilities supply shared C
lexing, command logging and digest helpers. Bytecode writes are disabled.
No existing TX test, release/lock input, target build, make or device is used.

Complete actual function bodies are extracted for global init/cleanup,
IRQ request/reverse release, host init/open/close/deinit/port retirement,
probe/abort/static-context clear, physical remove, callback admission/drain,
quarantine, late open/early close control, initial takeover and whole NAPI
poll/IRQ handlers. Complete architecture/register/dataplane headers are used
with only kernel include directives removed. FRAGMENTS.json binds each
original body and line to its prepared source; the generated C is retained.
A single scheduling hook is inserted at the start of the successful NAPI
completion branch to pause its post-completion tail. Kernel/SDK include/type
qualification is not claimed from mocked nss_dp_dev/netdev/HAL declarations.

Coverage includes global allocation failures and retry, every physical
probe failure stage, PHY-handle/fixed-link/MDIO and VSI/register failures,
every IRQ lookup/request position for stock one-ring and synthetic three-ring
classes, exact reverse acquisition/dev_id ledger, peer survival, duplicate
open/close/deinit, both partial-open/early-close quarantine paths, no guessed
NSS close, static-context pointer equality, all six unique takeover IDs,
overridden-ID remove/reprobe rejection, override-retained MMIO followed by
final port disable/unmap and repeat-exit no-MMIO behavior. Counts never make
failed probe attempts into takeover events. Re-probe after an actual failure
is distinguished from forbidden NSS-to-host ownership recovery.

Deterministic pthread schedules hold a real admitted plane_get snapshot
across quarantine, hold a port read across abort while a peer remains active,
and hold the full poll tail after NAPI ownership has been released. Retirement
must wait for the corresponding modeled drain; the final interrupt mask must
win over the old poll tail. napi_complete=false does not rearm; true does.
Source mutants must compile cleanly and fail their intended semantic stderr
gate, never merely time out. The follow-up gate requires exactly exit 86,
empty stdout and one complete expected diagnostic line, rejecting any extra
diagnostic. The positive suite must match the complete fixed summary, including
case/check counts and NAPI/IRQ ledgers. Two valid and sixteen result-pollution
controls exercise these gates; they are not extra driver scenarios or proof
that the semantic fixture itself is complete. Frozen r5 used the older
contains-marker gate and cannot be cited as exercising this follow-up.
Positive compile stdout/stderr and candidate
stderr must be empty. ASan/UBSan canaries require positive diagnostics, with
halt_on_error=1. LSan is disabled for host capability; explicit ledgers track
the resource classes modeled by this fixture.

Important exclusions
--------------------
The RCU, NAPI, waitqueue, netdev, IRQ, OF pdata and hardware-init callees are
explicit host models, not running Linux implementations. The actual RX DMA
cleaner is checked for RCU dereference statically but is not executed here;
queue-wake and full-poll reader paths are executed. free_irq synchronization
is an official kernel API contract, not reproduced as physical interrupts.
Synthetic ring IDs/counts do not qualify additional hardware configurations.
The inherited uint8_t PHY-id buffer requires -Wno-pointer-sign for its old
char* call sites; other enabled warnings remain fatal and logs must be empty.

In particular, edma_of_get_pdata and edma_hw_init are stubs in this CPU suite.
Passing failure/retry cases DO NOT certify OF node/device reference balance,
real allocation rollback or DMA idle. The known inherited EDMA OF double-get
without matching puts is an explicit P2 outside frozen 015, not a pass hidden
by the pdata stub. Common MDIO reference ownership is also outside scope.

No new all-slot DMA sweep, FW reset/idle guess or ring/IRQ/budget tuning is
introduced. External NSS/SSDK caches and asynchronous PHY-owned references,
hardware stop/completion visibility and duplicate opaque epochs remain OPEN.
Normal NSS-to-EDMA runtime restoration is deliberately not supported.

The final 014+015 TX function/header combination is delegated to the parent's
independent integration runner. This lifetime suite neither runs the original
014 runner (which stops at patch 014) nor borrows its PASS as combination
evidence. Its own RESULTS.json explicitly records that separation.
